<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deductibles.</strong> A wind/hail deductible may apply to the whole storm loss, while some policies apply a different deductible to other perils. Check your declarations page.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Depreciation is often withheld until repairs are completed. Track repair deadlines in your policy.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> Repairs must meet McAllen&rsquo;s 2024 ICC codes, effective January 1, 2026. Upgrade costs depend on ordinance or law coverage. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits and sublimits.</strong> Mold, debris removal, trees, and other structures often carry separate caps.</li>
<li><strong>Loss of use / business income.</strong> If the property is unlivable or a business closes, additional living expense or business interruption coverage may apply.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to Expect From the Insurer After a Storm</h2>
<p>Texas law sets general timelines for how insurers handle claims under the Prompt Payment of Claims Act (Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542). In general:</p>
<ul>
<li>The insurer has <strong>15 days</strong> after notice of the claim to acknowledge it, begin investigating, and request needed information.</li>
<li>After receiving all requested items, it generally has <strong>15 business days</strong> to accept or reject the claim, or it may notify you that it needs more time, up to <strong>45 additional days</strong>.</li>
<li>Payment is generally due within <strong>5 business days</strong> after the insurer notifies you it will pay.</li>
<li>These deadlines are extended by <strong>15 days</strong> after a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the insurance commissioner.</li>
</ul>
<p>Surplus lines insurers may follow different timeframes. You can read the statute on the <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Legislature website</a>. After a widespread hail or wind event, many claims are filed at once, and delays are common. Keeping a written log of every call, inspection, and request helps you track where your claim stands.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster can document each cause of loss, connect interior water to storm-created openings, gather weather evidence, prepare a combined estimate, and communicate with the insurer on your behalf. For businesses, see <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a>. If your claim has stalled, see <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a>. We do not guarantee outcomes; coverage depends on your policy and the facts. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Give prompt notice of the loss.</li>
<li>Mitigate: tarp openings, extract water, and start drying quickly to limit mold.</li>
<li>Keep damaged items until the insurer has inspected them or released them.</li>
<li>Complete a proof of loss if requested, within the policy deadline.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections and reasonable information requests.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Storm claims are commonly limited by flood and surface water exclusions, interior rain provisions that require a storm-created opening, wear and tear and deterioration exclusions, mold sublimits, cosmetic damage endorsements, and continuous or repeated seepage exclusions. Policies may also impose a contractual deadline to file suit, and weather-related lawsuits in Texas require written pre-suit notice. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
